single("s1-q01", "Prompt format", "A claims service calls a Foundation Model API chat endpoint and parses the reply into four named fields. About 3% of replies break the parser with missing or renamed keys. You can change any request parameter. What should you do?",
 ["Send the four fields as a `json_schema` response_format", "Send a `json_object` response_format with the fields", "Tell the model in the system prompt to always reply in valid JSON", "Lower the temperature to zero and retry on failures"], 1,
 "it guarantees valid JSON, but not your four keys; missing or renamed keys can still happen.",
 T("That asking for JSON is the same as enforcing a schema. Only `json_schema` constrains the field names.",
   [["Valid JSON but wrong keys", "No schema enforcement", "`json_schema` with the fields"], ["Structure unknown in advance", "Need any JSON", "`json_object`"], ["Can't touch request fields", "Prompt only", "Example output plus allowed values"]],
   [["\"missing or renamed keys\"", "The problem is the schema, not JSON validity."], ["\"You can change any request parameter\"", "So use the API feature, not prompting."]],
   "Known structure that code reads: `response_format` type `json_schema`.", O1),
 "Give the model a form with labelled boxes, not a blank page that says 'use boxes'.", SO,
 why=["Wins: `json_schema` constrains the reply to your field names and types, which fixes missing and renamed keys.",
      "Loses: `json_object` only promises syntactically valid JSON; it doesn't enforce your four keys.",
      "Loses: a prompt instruction lowers the error rate but enforces nothing, and the stem lets you use the API feature.",
      "Loses: temperature zero makes output more repeatable, not schema-correct; retries add cost and latency."]),
